$DIR/main.p:18:9: cannot return fmt.Errorf("conn: %w", c.eb.Errorf("x %d", 1)), which points into c, a local variable of returned
    fmt.Errorf("conn: %w", c.eb.Errorf("x %d", 1)) points into c. c is a local
    variable of returned (declared at main.p:17). Its memory is reclaimed when
    returned returns, so fmt.Errorf("conn: %w", c.eb.Errorf("x %d", 1)) would
    point at memory that no longer holds c.
    Go would move this memory to the heap, and its garbage collector would free
    it later. Plutonium has no garbage collector and never moves memory to the
    heap behind the program's back, so that every allocation is visible in the
    code.
    Return values instead of views of c, or allocate c and return its owner, an
    owned[Conn]:
        c := new(Conn)
        ...
        return c
    (see "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3001" for a longer explanation)

$DIR/main.p:23:2: cannot store fmt.Errorf("conn: %w", c.eb.Errorf("x %d", 1)) in package variable sink, which outlives c
    fmt.Errorf("conn: %w", c.eb.Errorf("x %d", 1)) points into c. c is a local
    variable of stored (declared at main.p:22). When stored returns, c's memory
    is reclaimed, but package variables live for the whole program, so package
    variable sink would be left pointing at reclaimed memory.
    Go would move this memory to the heap, and its garbage collector would free
    it later. Plutonium has no garbage collector and never moves memory to the
    heap behind the program's back, so that every allocation is visible in the
    code.
    Store values instead of views of c, or allocate c and store its owner, an
    owned[Conn], which moves there:
        sink = new(Conn)
    (see "Views" in the Plutonium spec, section 18.3)
    (run "pluto explain E3002" for a longer explanation)
